Step 3: Configure GitHub Pages deployment permissions

1. Return to the repository.
2. Click Settings.
3. Select Pages from the left sidebar.
4. Under Build and deployment, locate the Source option.
5. Select GitHub Actions if that option is available.

This allows a GitHub Actions workflow to deploy the website.

Step 4: Create the deployment workflow

1. Return to the Code tab.
2. Click Add file → Create new file.
3. Enter the following filename:

".github/workflows/deploy.yml"

4. Paste the complete workflow below.

6. Program: deploy.yml

name: Deploy Static Website

on:
  push:
    branches:
      - main
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read
  pages: write
  id-token: write

concurrency:
  group: pages
  cancel-in-progress: true

jobs:
  deploy:
    environment:
      name: github-pages
      url: ${{ steps.deployment.outputs.page_url }}

    runs-on: ubuntu-latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout repository
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Configure GitHub Pages
        uses: actions/configure-pages@v5

      - name: Upload website files
        uses: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v3
        with:
          path: .

      - name: Deploy website
        id: deployment
        uses: actions/deploy-pages@v4

5. Scroll down to Commit changes.
6. Enter the commit message "Add static website deployment workflow".
7. Commit the file to main.

Important: This workflow uploads the repository contents as the website artifact. For a real project, keep the website files in a dedicated folder or configure the artifact path to include only the intended site files.

Step 5: Understand the workflow

- "name" specifies the workflow name.
- "on.push" triggers deployment when changes are pushed to main.
- "workflow_dispatch" allows manual execution.
- "permissions" grants the workflow the permissions needed to publish the site.
- "concurrency" helps prevent overlapping deployments.
- "actions/checkout@v4" checks out the repository.
- "actions/configure-pages@v5" configures GitHub Pages.
- "actions/upload-pages-artifact@v3" packages the website files.
- "actions/deploy-pages@v4" publishes the website.

